{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3060","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-runtime-hints-r7.md@sha256:2ab1e4c020a17af8399ffe5b119a201c50c51e4a2113f08a22104c4787df6479"},"owner_reference":"3060","content":"# Architecture Spec: R7-Compliant Runtime Hints in `backlog_view`\n\nBacklog item: #3060 \u2014 \"fix: runtime hints steer agents away from paginated navigation\"\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\nTwo hint-construction functions in `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` violate\nR7 (\"Hints must be actionable\") of\n`plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`:\n\n- `_build_compact_manifest()`'s `_hint` field (server.py:2275-2314) recommends an unqualified\n  full-content load (`summary=False`) as its *first* suggestion.\n- `_build_over_budget_view()`'s `_usage` field (server.py:2352-2392) unconditionally repeats\n  \"narrow to a section\" advice even when the caller already narrowed to a single section that is\n  itself over budget \u2014 advice already exhausted.\n\nThe fix is two isolated string-construction changes plus one signature addition, entirely inside\n`server.py`. No change to `disclosure_handler.py`, `ordinal_mapper.py`, or `content_normalizer.py`\nis required or made \u2014 those are read-only reference material for this design.\n\nAn open design question from RT-ICA reconciliation (2026-08-21) \u2014 whether `navigate=<ordinal>` can\nbe safely named in `_build_over_budget_view()`'s `_usage` using an ordinal drawn from its\n`sections_index` \u2014 is resolved below with evidence: **no**. The response names `map=True` instead\n(Candidate Resolution 2 from the RT-ICA reconciliation entry).\n\n## 2. Resolution: The Ordinal-Compatibility Open Question\n\n### 2.1 The question\n\nCan `_build_over_budget_view()`'s `_usage` string validly name `navigate=<N>` using one of the\n`[N]` bracket numbers from its own `sections_index` field (built by `_sections_index_from_result()`,\nserver.py:2317)? Those numbers must be genuinely resolvable by\n`OrdinalPathMapper.resolve()`/`valid_ordinals()` (`backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py`) \u2014 the mechanism\n`navigate=` actually dispatches to (`disclosure_handler.py`'s `BacklogViewDisclosureHandler`) \u2014 or\nnaming `navigate=` is itself a new R7 violation (prohibition 3: never name a mechanism/address\nabsent from the response it accompanies).\n\n### 2.2 Evidence\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 `_build_over_budget_view()` runs only on the `summary=False` branch, where\n`_sections_index_from_result()` uses its dict-`enumerate()` fallback, not the `[N]`-line source \u2014\nincluding in AC2's own single-section-narrowed scenario.**\n\n`_build_over_budget_view()` is called from exactly one call site (server.py:2681), nested inside\n`backlog_view()`'s `if not summary:` block (server.py:2626) \u2014 every branch of that block returns,\nso `_build_over_budget_view()` is reachable **only** when `summary=False`. (`_build_compact_manifest()`,\nby contrast, is reached only when `summary=True`, at the `if not summary:` block's implicit\nfall-through, server.py:2683 \u2014 the two functions are mutually exclusive per call.) `include_content`\ndefaults to `True` independently of `summary` (server.py `backlog_view()` parameter default,\n~line 2444), so the common `backlog_view(selector=X, summary=False)` call still takes the\nfull-content path in `operations.py`.\n\nThis holds specifically for AC2's own scenario (`section=<a single section that is itself over the\ntoken budget>`), not just the general case: reading `operations.py`'s content-population function\n(~line 2736-2778), the `pending_index`/`result.sections_index` assignment only happens in the\n`elif not paginate:` branch that runs when **`section is None`** (line 2776-2778). When\n`section is not None` (AC2's scenario), that branch is skipped \u2014 `result.sections_index` is never\nset, staying empty \u2014 while `result.sections` IS narrowed at line 2763-2764\n(`if not paginate: result.sections = _build_sections_metadata(body, show, since, section=None)`,\nbuilt from the body `_apply_body_section_filter()` already narrowed at line 2744). So in AC2's exact\nscenario, `_sections_index_from_result()` (server.py:2317-2349) skips its first branch\n(`if result.sections_index: return result.sections_index`, empty here) and falls through to:\n\n```python\nfor idx, (name, sec) in enumerate(result.sections.items()):\n    ...\n```\n\n\u2014 i.e. the `[N]` numbers shown to the caller are `dict.items()` enumeration positions over\n`ViewItemResult.sections`, a plain `dict[str, ...]` (already narrowed to the one matched section in\nthis scenario, per the paragraph above \u2014 this is what makes the `narrowed_to_single_section`\ndetection in \u00a74.2 correct: `result.sections`/`full_response[\"sections\"]` genuinely reflects the\nsingular-`section=` narrowing, confirmed by this exact code path, not merely assumed from the\n`_apply_body_section_filter` docstring's name).\n\n**Finding 2 \u2014 `OrdinalPathMapper`'s ordinals are assigned by a structurally different mechanism\nthat explicitly rejects dict-order as authoritative.**\n\n`navigate=` ordinals are resolved by `OrdinalPathMapper.resolve()`\n(`backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py:459-495`) against a resolution index built by `build_map()`\n(`ordinal_mapper.py:327-421`), which assigns `level1_ordinal = str(section.index)` from a\n`list[NormalizedSection]`. That list is produced by `ItemContentNormalizer.normalize()`\n(`backlog_core/content_normalizer.py:157-196`), whose own module docstring states the design\nintent directly: *\"Canonical order always derives from `[N] Title` lines in the sections index,\nnever from `dict.keys()` iteration.\"* `normalize()` implements this by parsing `[N] Title` lines\nout of `result.sections_index or result.body` (content_normalizer.py:183-184) via\n`_parse_section_titles()`, assigning `idx = len(out)` sequentially per line parsed \u2014 a **third**,\nindependently implemented enumeration, sharing no code with `_sections_index_from_result()`'s\ndict-`enumerate()` fallback.\n\n**Finding 3 \u2014 the two enumerations are provably non-equivalent in a case the codebase itself\ndocuments.** `ItemContentNormalizer.normalize()`'s own docstring (content_normalizer.py:163-169)\nstates: *\"When the sections index contains duplicate titles ... both produce `NormalizedSection`\nentries with the same content from `sections_dict` but different list-position ordinals ... the\nsections dict collapses duplicates.\"* A `dict[str, ...]` cannot hold two entries under the same\nkey; two same-titled sections collapse to one dict entry, shifting every subsequent\n`enumerate(result.sections.items())` index left of where the document-order `[N]` parse (and hence\nevery `OrdinalPathMapper` ordinal) places it. This is not a hypothetical edge case reasoned about\nin the abstract \u2014 it is the exact scenario the normalizer's docstring was written to document,\nproving the two numbering schemes are not the same value space by construction, not merely\n\"unverified to be the same.\" Confirmed independently by reading the `[N]`-line builder itself:\n`_build_sections_index_from_body()` (operations.py:1905-1932) enumerates `_build_sections_compact(body)`,\na `list`, via plain `enumerate(sections)` \u2014 a list preserves every entry including duplicate names,\nso its `[N]` numbering does not collapse the way the `result.sections` **dict** (keyed by name) does.\nTwo independently-typed collections (list vs. name-keyed dict) processing the same document is\nexactly the shape of bug the codebase's own docstring already flags.\n\n**Finding 4 \u2014 `_build_compact_manifest()`'s `sections_index` carries the identical exposure.**\n`_build_compact_manifest()` also calls `_sections_index_from_result(result)`\n(server.py:2311-2313) on a `result` fetched under `summary=True`, where `include_content` still\ndefaults to `True` independently of `summary` (server.py `backlog_view()` parameter default,\n~line 2444) \u2014 the full-content path. The same dict-`enumerate()` fallback fires there too (by the\nidentical reasoning as Finding 1, minus the `section is not None` branch specifics, which do not\napply to the unfiltered default `summary=True` call). This is why the `_hint` fix (\u00a73) does not add\na `navigate=` suggestion either \u2014 doing so would introduce the identical unverified-ordinal problem\nin a second location.\n\n**Finding 5 \u2014 `map=True` is unconditionally reachable and self-consistent, independent of the\nPASSTHROUGH branch that produced the over-budget response.** `map=True` is parsed by\n`DisclosureRequestParser.parse(map=True)` into `DisclosureMode.MAP`\n(`disclosure_handler.py:169-172`) and dispatched by `BacklogViewDisclosureHandler.handle()`\n(`disclosure_handler.py:303-374`), which performs its **own** fresh, un-gated\n`operations.view_item(selector)` fetch, its own `ItemContentNormalizer.normalize()`, and its own\n`OrdinalPathMapper(sections).build_map()` (`disclosure_handler.py:330-335`) \u2014 entirely independent\nof whatever `result`/`sections_index` the legacy PASSTHROUGH over-budget branch computed. `map=True`\nrequires only `selector` (already available and already used to build `_usage`) \u2014 no ordinal\nargument \u2014 so it sidesteps the addressing-mismatch question entirely rather than resolving it.\nThis matches the `backlog_view` tool's own docstring, which documents `map=True` as step 1 of the\nthree-layer progressive-disclosure pattern precisely for this \"I don't know the structure yet\"\nmoment.\n\n**Caveat found during verification (not blocking, logged for tracking):** no code path found in\n`server.py`, `disclosure_handler.py`, or `disclosure_types.py` enforces the tool docstring's claim\nthat a `map=True` response is \"always under 2,000 tokens\" \u2014 `_handle_map()`\n(`disclosure_handler.py:376-403`) builds `map_text` from every `OrdinalEntry` with no truncation\nstep; the guarantee appears to rely entirely on `format_map_line()`'s per-line title/preview caps\n(`ordinal_mapper.py:423-457`, 50/60 chars) holding in practice, not on an enforced budget gate. This\nis a pre-existing, unrelated discrepancy between documented and enforced behavior \u2014 out of scope\nfor this fix (Anti-Goal 2: not touching disclosure_handler.py internals or trigger thresholds) but\nworth a follow-up backlog item; it does not change the resolution below, since Candidate 2 does not\ndepend on the 2,000-token claim holding, only on `map=True` being reachable and useful.\n\n### 2.3 Resolution \u2014 Candidate 2: name `map=True`, not `navigate=<N>`\n\n`_build_over_budget_view()`'s `_usage` names `backlog_view(selector='{selector}', map=True)` as\nthe next call when the caller has already narrowed to a single section and is still over budget. It\nnever names a `navigate=` ordinal. This satisfies R7 prohibition 3 unconditionally: `map=True`\ncarries no address, so there is nothing to be invalid.\n\nCandidates 1 and 3 are rejected:\n\n- **Candidate 1** (give the over-budget response a genuine ordinal, or invoke\n  `OrdinalPathMapper.build_map()` inline) would require `_build_over_budget_view()` to perform the\n  same un-gated `operations.view_item()` + `ItemContentNormalizer` + `OrdinalPathMapper` pipeline\n  `BacklogViewDisclosureHandler.handle()` already performs for `map=True` \u2014 i.e. reimplementing\n  `map=True`'s own code path a second time inside the legacy PASSTHROUGH branch. This duplicates\n  Finding 5's mechanism instead of reusing it, and is explicitly out of scope under Anti-Goal 2\n  (\"not touching pagination-cache or control-set code\") and Anti-Goal 3 (\"not adding a new\n  navigation mechanism\").\n- **Candidate 3** (name `navigate=` only when a genuinely resolvable ordinal is present) requires\n  first computing whether one is present \u2014 which requires the same `OrdinalPathMapper` build that\n  Candidate 1 does, for no benefit over just calling `map=True` directly.\n\n## 3. `_build_compact_manifest()` \u2014 `_hint` Fix\n\n**File**: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`\n**Function**: `_build_compact_manifest()` (~line 2275-2314)\n**Change scope**: `_hint` field value only \u2014 no signature change, no other field changes.\n\n### 3.1 Current\n\n```python\n\"_hint\": (\n    f\"Load full content: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False)\\n\"\n    f\"Load specific sections: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='<index, title, or /regex/>')\"\n),\n```\n\n### 3.2 New\n\n```python\n\"_hint\": (\n    f\"Load specific sections: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='<index, title, or /regex/>')\\n\"\n    f\"Load full content: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False)\"\n),\n```\n\nThe two lines are swapped, unchanged otherwise. This is the minimal change that satisfies AC1\n(\"first recommended call is a section-narrowing call \u2014 not `summary=False`\") \u2014 a section-narrowing\nsuggestion now leads; the unqualified full-content load is demoted to the fallback position R7\npermits (\"must never recommend retrieving everything as its **first** suggestion\" \u2014 not \"must never\nmention it\").\n\n### 3.3 Explicitly not adding `navigate=`/`map=` here\n\nPer Finding 4 (\u00a72.2), `_build_compact_manifest()`'s own `sections_index` field carries the same\ndict-`enumerate()`-fallback exposure as `_build_over_budget_view()`'s. Adding a `navigate=<N>`\nsuggestion to `_hint` would repeat the exact defect this fix resolves elsewhere. The reconciled\nAcceptance Criteria (AC1) requires only the reordering above \u2014 it does not require `_hint` to\nmention pagination \u2014 so this fix does not introduce that additional surface. (Discovery's Goal 3,\npredating the RT-ICA reconciliation, is superseded here by this finding; do not add `navigate=`/\n`map=` to `_hint` in a later pass without re-deriving section-count reliability for the\n`include_content=True` default path first.)\n\n### 3.4 Substring/field preservation (verifies AC4, Tests \u00a7)\n\n- `\"summary=False\"` still present (second line) \u2014 preserves\n  `test_backlog_view_summary_true_returns_compact_manifest`.\n- `{selector}` still present verbatim in both lines \u2014 preserves\n  `test_backlog_view_summary_true_hint_contains_selector`.\n- No top-level key added/removed \u2014 preserves `test_backlog_view_disclosure_tool.py`'s\n  `_PASSTHROUGH_LEGACY_KEYS` assertion.\n\n## 4. `_build_over_budget_view()` \u2014 Signature Change and `_usage` Fix\n\n**File**: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`\n**Function**: `_build_over_budget_view()` (~line 2352-2392)\n**Call site**: inside `backlog_view()`, PASSTHROUGH branch (~line 2680-2681)\n\n### 4.1 Signature change\n\n```python\n# Current\ndef _build_over_budget_view(result: _models.ViewItemResult, full_chars: int, selector: str) -> dict[str, object]:\n\n# New\ndef _build_over_budget_view(\n    result: _models.ViewItemResult,\n    full_chars: int,\n    selector: str,\n    *,\n    narrowed_to_single_section: bool = False,\n) -> dict[str, object]:\n```\n\n`narrowed_to_single_section` is keyword-only with a default of `False` so every existing\n3-positional-argument call site (including both cited unit tests, which construct\n`_build_over_budget_view(result, full_chars, selector)` directly) is unaffected and continues to\nreceive the unchanged, unconditional \"narrow with sections=\" `_usage` text \u2014 this is what keeps\n`test_build_over_budget_view_usage_mentions_sections_param` and\n`test_build_over_budget_view_includes_all_nine_fields` (AC4) passing with no test edits required.\n\n### 4.2 Call-site signal computation\n\nThe signal is derived entirely from locals already resolved in `backlog_view()`'s body by the time\n`_build_over_budget_view()` is called (RT-ICA reconciliation condition 4: `section` and\n`sections_filter` are in scope; this spec additionally uses `full_response[\"sections\"]`, the\nalready-filtered dict, rather than `result.sections`, because `_filter_view_sections()` mutates\n`full_response` in place for the plural `sections=[...]` path but does **not** mutate `result`\n(`_filter_view_sections()` docstring, server.py:1240-1249) \u2014 `result.sections` alone would\ntherefore miss the plural-narrowing case).\n\n```python\nserialised = _json.dumps(full_response)\nif _view_payload_token_count(full_response) > _VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET:\n    narrowed_sections = full_response.get(\"sections\")\n    narrowed_to_single_section = bool(\n        (section is not None or sections_filter is not None)\n        and isinstance(narrowed_sections, dict)\n        and len(narrowed_sections) == 1\n    )\n    return _build_over_budget_view(\n        result, len(serialised), selector, narrowed_to_single_section=narrowed_to_single_section\n    )\n```\n\nRequiring both \"caller explicitly requested narrowing\" (`section is not None or sections_filter is\nnot None`) **and** \"exactly one section remains after that narrowing\" precisely captures AC2's\nscenario (`section=<a single section that is itself over the token budget>`) while leaving the\nun-narrowed default response (item happens to have exactly one section, caller passed no filter)\non the existing, still-technically-actionable \"try sections=\" text \u2014 that combination has not\nactually been exhausted by the caller, so recommending it is not an R7 prohibition-2 violation, and\nchanging that case is not required by any AC.\n\n### 4.3 New `_usage` content\n\n```python\n\"_usage\": (\n    _build_narrowed_usage(selector, full_chars)\n    if narrowed_to_single_section\n    else (\n        f\"This response exceeded the {_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET}-token budget \"\n        f\"({full_chars} chars in full form). \"\n        \"Use the sections_index below to identify which sections you need, \"\n        \"then request them individually:\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, sections=['Section Name'])\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='0,1,3')\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='/regex/')\"\n    )\n),\n```\n\nWhere the narrowed branch reads:\n\n```python\ndef _build_narrowed_usage(selector: str, full_chars: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Build the _usage string for an over-budget response already narrowed to one section.\n\n    Section-based narrowing (section=/sections=) has already been applied and is\n    still over budget for the single section that remains \u2014 repeating that advice\n    would violate R7 (never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted).\n    Names map=True instead: it takes no address argument, so it cannot itself\n    violate R7's third prohibition (never name a mechanism/address absent from the\n    response it accompanies) \u2014 see architect-runtime-hints-r7.md \u00a72 for the\n    evidence that a navigate=<ordinal> suggestion here would be unverified.\n\n    Args:\n        selector: Original selector string used to build the usage hint.\n        full_chars: Character length of the serialised full response.\n\n    Returns:\n        Usage string naming map=True as the next call.\n    \"\"\"\n    return (\n        f\"This response exceeded the {_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET}-token budget \"\n        f\"({full_chars} chars in full form), even after narrowing to a single section \u2014 \"\n        \"further section= narrowing is not available for this request. \"\n        \"Get this item's ordinal map, then page through the oversized section with \"\n        \"navigate=/head=/skip_tokens=:\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', map=True)\"\n    )\n```\n\n(Splitting the narrowed-branch text into its own function is a documentation/readability choice,\nnot a requirement \u2014 an inline conditional expression is equally acceptable if the implementer\nprefers to keep the diff to one function. Either form must produce the same string content.)\n\nThis text:\n\n- Does not repeat `sections=`/`section=` advice (R7 prohibition 2 \u2014 satisfies AC2).\n- Names exactly one call, `backlog_view(selector='{selector}', map=True)`, which is unconditionally\n  valid \u2014 no address is asserted, so R7 prohibition 3 cannot be violated (satisfies AC3).\n- Mentions `navigate=`/`head=`/`skip_tokens=` in prose as the mechanism reached *after* the `map=True`\n  call returns ordinals to navigate with \u2014 it does not name a concrete ordinal value, so it makes no\n  claim AC3 could falsify (satisfies Goal 3's request to reference the pagination parameters without\n  reintroducing the ordinal-validity problem Finding 3 identified).\n\n### 4.4 Substring/field preservation (verifies AC4, Tests \u00a7)\n\n- Un-narrowed branch text is byte-identical to the current text \u2014 `\"sections=\"` and `{selector}`\n  both still present \u2014 preserves `test_build_over_budget_view_usage_mentions_sections_param`.\n- No top-level key added/removed in either branch \u2014 preserves\n  `test_build_over_budget_view_includes_all_nine_fields` and `test_backlog_view_disclosure_tool.py`'s\n  `_PASSTHROUGH_LEGACY_KEYS` assertion.\n\n## 5. Test Design (AC6 \u2014 new tests required)\n\nBoth new tests are unit-level, calling the two builder functions directly (matching the existing\ndirect-call convention already used by\n`test_backlog_view_summary_true_returns_compact_manifest`,\n`test_build_over_budget_view_usage_mentions_sections_param`, etc. in\n`plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py`) \u2014 no live backend required.\n\n### 5.1 `_build_compact_manifest()` ordering test\n\n```python\ndef test_compact_manifest_hint_leads_with_section_narrowing() -> None:\n    \"\"\"_hint's first line must be a section-narrowing call, not summary=False (AC1).\"\"\"\n    result = <ViewItemResult fixture with a non-empty sections_index / sections dict>\n    manifest = _build_compact_manifest(result, full_response={}, selector=\"#3060\")\n    first_line = manifest[\"_hint\"].split(\"\\n\", 1)[0]\n    unqualified_full_load = \"Load full content: backlog_view(selector='#3060', summary=False)\"\n    assert \"section=\" in first_line\n    assert first_line != unqualified_full_load\n```\n\n### 5.2 `_build_over_budget_view()` narrowed-branch unit test (direct call)\n\n```python\ndef test_over_budget_view_narrowed_usage_names_map_not_sections() -> None:\n    \"\"\"Narrowed-to-one-section _usage must not repeat sections= advice and must name\n    a resolvable mechanism (AC2, AC3).\"\"\"\n    result = <ViewItemResult fixture>\n    view = _build_over_budget_view(\n        result, full_chars=99999, selector=\"#3060\", narrowed_to_single_section=True\n    )\n    usage = view[\"_usage\"]\n    assert \"sections=\" not in usage\n    assert \"map=True\" in usage\n    assert \"#3060\" in usage\n    # No actionable section=/sections= call expression appears \u2014 a prose mention of\n    # \"section=\" inside \"further section= narrowing is not available\" is acceptable;\n    # assert against the literal call-expression forms, not the bare substring:\n    assert \"sections=['\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='0\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='/\" not in usage\n```\n\nThis unit test proves the `_usage` text is correct once `narrowed_to_single_section=True` is\npassed. It does **not** prove the call site in \u00a74.2 actually computes `True` for a real narrowed\nrequest \u2014 that requires a second, request-level test.\n\n### 5.3 `_build_over_budget_view()` narrowed-branch integration test (request-level, required)\n\nAC6(b) is phrased at the request level (\"the over-budget `_usage` **for a request** that already\nnarrowed\"), and the call-site predicate in \u00a74.2 is exactly the part 5.2 cannot exercise (it bypasses\n`backlog_view()` entirely). Add a second test that drives the real tool end to end against a fixture\nitem with a section whose own content alone still exceeds `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`:\n\n```python\nasync def test_backlog_view_section_narrowed_over_budget_names_map(monkeypatch) -> None:\n    \"\"\"A section= request that narrows to one section and is still over budget must\n    receive a _usage naming map=True, not repeated sections=/section= advice (AC2).\"\"\"\n    # Fixture: an item whose single named section's content alone exceeds\n    # _VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET (e.g. a long generated body), reachable via the same\n    # test-fixture mechanism test_view_over_budget_gate.py already uses.\n    response = await backlog_view(selector=\"<fixture selector>\", summary=False, section=\"<one oversized section>\")\n    assert response[\"_over_budget\"] is True\n    usage = response[\"_usage\"]\n    assert \"map=True\" in usage\n    assert \"sections=['\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='0\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='/\" not in usage\n```\n\nIf this test fails to observe `narrowed_to_single_section` evaluating `True` at the call site (i.e.\n`map=True` does not appear), the \u00a74.2 predicate has a defect and must be corrected before this fix\nis considered complete \u2014 do not adjust the assertion to match incorrect behavior.\n\n### 5.4 Existing suite\n\nNo changes required to any of the 5 files cited in Acceptance Criteria item 5\n(`test_backlog_core_server.py`, `test_backlog_view_disclosure_tool.py`,\n`test_view_over_budget_gate.py`, `test_view_pagination_section_index.py`,\n`test_section_filter_miss.py`) beyond adding the two tests above \u2014 per \u00a73.4 and \u00a74.4, every existing\nsubstring/field-name assertion continues to pass unmodified.\n\n## 6. Module Boundaries (unchanged \u2014 confirms Impact Radius Files table)\n\n| File | This fix | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| `backlog_core/server.py` | **Change target** | Both hint-construction functions and the one call site live here |\n| `backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py` | Read-only reference | `map=True`/`navigate=`/`_handle_extract()`'s `next_call` pattern already correct; not touched |\n| `backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py` | Read-only reference | Consulted only to prove the ordinal-compatibility question (\u00a72) |\n| `backlog_core/content_normalizer.py` | Read-only reference | Consulted only to prove the ordinal-compatibility question (\u00a72) |\n| `docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` | Not touched | States R7; no wording change needed |\n\n## 7. ADR \u2014 Why `map=True` Over a New Ordinal-Building Path\n\n**Decision**: `_build_over_budget_view()`'s narrowed-branch `_usage` names `map=True`, never\n`navigate=<N>`.\n\n**Context**: See \u00a72. Two independently implemented section-numbering schemes coexist in\n`backlog_core/`: a dict-`enumerate()` fallback (`_sections_index_from_result()`) and a\ndocument-order `[N]`-line parse (`ItemContentNormalizer` \u2192 `OrdinalPathMapper`). They are proven\nnon-equivalent under duplicate section titles.\n\n**Alternatives considered**: give `_build_over_budget_view()` its own `OrdinalPathMapper` build\n(rejected \u2014 duplicates `map=True`'s own code path inside the legacy PASSTHROUGH branch, violating\nAnti-Goals 2 and 3); conditionally name `navigate=` only when independently verified resolvable\n(rejected \u2014 requires the same build as the first alternative for no benefit).\n\n**Consequence**: the fix stays inside `server.py`, touches no shared disclosure code, and never\nasserts an address it cannot guarantee. A future fix that unifies the two numbering schemes (e.g.\nmaking `_sections_index_from_result()` always source from the `[N]`-line parse instead of falling\nback to dict-`enumerate()`) would be a separate, larger change \u2014 worth a follow-up backlog item,\nnot part of this fix's scope (Anti-Goal 2/3).\n"}